.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR_MP 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr_mp \- declare a raw pointer freed automatically at scope exit via a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <common.h>

ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(name, type, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares "type *name", initialised to NULL, and registers automatic
cleanup via __attribute__((cleanup(...))). Assign to name normally (e.g.
via _mem_alloc(mmgmt_procs, size)); whichever value it holds when the
enclosing scope ends is freed via mmgmt_procs->free (or the default
allocator, via mem_free(), if mmgmt_procs is NULL).

This is the custom-allocator counterpart to ccol_scoped_ptr(); see that
page for the default-allocator form and for the caveats shared by both
(reassignment, and ownership release via ccol_scoped_ptr_release()).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name          Name of the pointer variable to declare
type          Pointee type (e.g. char, struct foo)
mmgmt_procs   Custom allocator (ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *) used to free
              name's final value, or NULL for the default allocator
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Requires GCC or Clang (uses __attribute__((cleanup)))

mmgmt_procs is stored by reference, not copied; it must remain valid
for at least as long as name's own enclosing scope. This holds
naturally whenever mmgmt_procs is itself a variable already in scope at
the point of declaration, which is the common case.

Passing the address of a local variable directly as mmgmt_procs (e.g.
&some_local_procs) can trip -Waddress ("the address of X will always
evaluate as true") under this library's own -Werror build flags, since
_mem_alloc/_mem_free's internal NULL check becomes provably always-true
at that call site. Store the address in a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t * variable
first and pass that instead.

Expands to two declarations, not one expression; use it as its own
statement, the same constraint every *_construct_scoped macro in this
library already has.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
The declared pointer itself has no synchronisation; it is a plain local
variable and follows ordinary C scoping and thread-local storage rules.
Thread safety of mmgmt_procs's own functions is the caller's
responsibility, exactly as for every other _mp-suffixed API in this
library.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(buf, char, mp);
  buf = _mem_alloc(mp, 128);
  if (!buf) return;

  /* buf is used here */

}  /* buf is freed via mp on every return path */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_release (3)
